WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:03.169
-Our next guest is a sports
Emmy Award-winning journalist

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:04.971
who hosts "Game Theory
with Bomani Jones."

00:00:04.971 --> 00:00:07.740
New episodes premiere Friday
nights on HBO and HBO Max.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.275
Please welcome to the show
Bomani Jones, everybody.

00:00:10.275 --> 00:00:12.278
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:00:12.278 --> 00:00:15.281
[ Rock plays ]

00:00:15.281 --> 00:00:23.823 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:23.823 --> 00:00:25.959
Welcome to the show, Bomani!
I'm so happy to you have here.

00:00:25.959 --> 00:00:27.293
-I appreciate it.
You give everybody

00:00:27.293 --> 00:00:28.928
the dap hug like that,
or am I special?

00:00:28.928 --> 00:00:30.864
-You're special. Yeah.
-Okay, just checking.

00:00:30.864 --> 00:00:32.398
[ Laughter ]
-You know what?
I don't know if I gave it

00:00:32.398 --> 00:00:34.934
to your sister
when she was here because --

00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:37.870
this is very fun --
your sister was the first

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:39.172
in your family
to be on the show.

00:00:39.172 --> 00:00:41.808
She's an author.
She wrote an incredible

00:00:41.808 --> 00:00:43.109
book called
"An American Marriage."

00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:45.011
-Yeah. I have to say,
I was very excited

00:00:45.011 --> 00:00:46.579
and proud of myself
until I realized

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:47.947
I was second place
in my own house

00:00:47.947 --> 00:00:49.248
[ Laughter ]
on being on this
television show, okay?

00:00:49.248 --> 00:00:51.350
-But you do have siblings
who haven't been on the show

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:53.453
as well, right?
-Yeah, I do. I do have
a couple of those, so.

00:00:53.453 --> 00:00:55.455
[ Laughter ]
-Okay, great. So you can
lord it over them.

00:00:55.455 --> 00:00:57.090
-Yeah. Like my brother lives
across the street from me.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:58.724
I will definitely have
to tell him that,

00:00:58.724 --> 00:01:00.160
"You're going to have
to figure something out."

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:01.594
He better start telling some
jokes or dancing or something.

00:01:01.594 --> 00:01:02.895
[ Laughter ]

00:01:02.895 --> 00:01:04.396
-Now, you have covered sports
for a long time

00:01:04.396 --> 00:01:06.532
-Yes.
-in a lot of different
formats --

00:01:06.532 --> 00:01:09.468
radio, podcasts, on TV --
but now you got your own show.

00:01:09.468 --> 00:01:11.904
And with the world of sports
being as big as it is,

00:01:11.904 --> 00:01:13.539
it must be cool
to finally be in a position

00:01:13.539 --> 00:01:15.674
to set the agenda every time
you get behind the camera.

00:01:15.674 --> 00:01:17.009
-Yeah, like I think when you do

00:01:17.009 --> 00:01:18.711
like a radio show
and stuff like that,

00:01:18.711 --> 00:01:21.014
you are in charge,
but it's radio, right?

00:01:21.014 --> 00:01:22.816
Like people don't take it nearly
as seriously.

00:01:22.816 --> 00:01:25.952
It's just a little bit of a zoo
of sorts when you do that.

00:01:25.952 --> 00:01:27.420
When you get into television,

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.722
it's a zillion different people
that are doing this, doing that.

00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:31.825
They got all these different
departments to get into.

00:01:31.825 --> 00:01:33.058
And I remember the first day

00:01:33.058 --> 00:01:34.927
that we opened the office up
for "Game Theory,"

00:01:34.927 --> 00:01:38.031
we had this meeting and people
were presenting the schematics

00:01:38.031 --> 00:01:39.765
for some field piece
that we had.

00:01:39.765 --> 00:01:41.800
And it dawned on me --
I've never been in a meeting

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:43.736
where everybody cares
about what I think

00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:45.705
and they're all staring at me
like so intently,

00:01:45.705 --> 00:01:47.474
trying to figure out how I feel
about something.

00:01:47.474 --> 00:01:49.475
But it's COVID,
so I got a mask on.

00:01:49.475 --> 00:01:50.676
[ Laughter ]
And they just lose it.

00:01:50.676 --> 00:01:52.378
Like they just staring
and they're so scared

00:01:52.378 --> 00:01:55.281
and I got this like giant smile
on my face the whole way

00:01:55.281 --> 00:01:57.517
and they have no idea because
we're trying not to die.

00:01:57.517 --> 00:01:58.818
[ Laughter ]

00:01:58.818 --> 00:02:01.187
-That was the right thing
to try not to do.

00:02:01.187 --> 00:02:02.822
One thing you and I have
in common --

00:02:02.822 --> 00:02:04.624
I've obviously been doing this
a long time,

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:06.825
you're new at it,
but one thing we agree on --

00:02:06.825 --> 00:02:08.360
we do not care for teleprompter.
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:09.596
-I've got a cue card guy.

00:02:09.596 --> 00:02:10.863
I've been working on cue cards
my whole career.

00:02:10.863 --> 00:02:12.798
But how are you finding
the teleprompter?

00:02:12.798 --> 00:02:14.167
Are you getting used to it?
-Yeah, man, I went in

00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:15.667
and got a teleprompter coach

00:02:15.667 --> 00:02:16.736
-Oh, yeah?
-to help me do that

00:02:16.736 --> 00:02:18.338
because like I could read
the teleprompter.

00:02:18.338 --> 00:02:20.339
It's just really hard
to be yourself

00:02:20.339 --> 00:02:21.807
while you're reading
the teleprompter.

00:02:21.807 --> 00:02:24.878
Every word that I'd ever said
in public before, really,

00:02:24.878 --> 00:02:26.579
was coming
off of the top of my head.

00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:28.480
And, now, all of a sudden
we have to write this down

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:30.416
and that feels confining
and everything else.

00:02:30.416 --> 00:02:32.418
You know, I've got somebody,
he's an actor and he comes in

00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:34.253
and he basically teaches me how
to read again.

00:02:34.253 --> 00:02:35.621
[ Laughter ]
-So you feel good?
-I do. I do.

00:02:35.621 --> 00:02:37.290
-Alright. I want to ask you
about some of the stuff

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:38.857
in the world
of sports right now.

00:02:38.857 --> 00:02:41.126
First off, I mean,
it does feel like maybe this

00:02:41.126 --> 00:02:42.862
is the third offseason in a row

00:02:42.862 --> 00:02:44.163
where this is going
to be a question.

00:02:44.163 --> 00:02:45.498
Tom Brady -- did he come back,
is he going to play

00:02:45.498 --> 00:02:47.166
another year?
What do you think?

00:02:47.166 --> 00:02:48.968
-I feel like,
if you put your whole family

00:02:48.968 --> 00:02:50.770
on the roulette wheel
to go 8-9

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:53.873
and now, that family isn't there
anymore, why not come back

00:02:53.873 --> 00:02:55.441
[ Laughter ]
-Oh, interesting.
-and play again, right?

00:02:55.441 --> 00:02:57.644
Like, "Sure, I'd love
to go sit in my house by myself

00:02:57.644 --> 00:02:59.011
and eat Hungry Man dinners.

00:02:59.011 --> 00:03:00.980
[ Laughter ]
Yeah. Who doesn't want
to do that?"

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:04.550
No, I mean, in fairness to him,
any football player I talked to,

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:06.986
the adjustment
of no longer playing anymore

00:03:06.986 --> 00:03:08.788
is really, really
difficult for them.

00:03:08.788 --> 00:03:10.823
And people will let Tom Brady
play football

00:03:10.823 --> 00:03:12.458
as long as he wants
to play football.

00:03:12.458 --> 00:03:13.793
I, personally, would not want

00:03:13.793 --> 00:03:16.062
to get hit in the kidneys
at age 46,

00:03:16.062 --> 00:03:17.396
but I got different kidneys
than he does.

00:03:17.396 --> 00:03:18.730
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:19.832
There's like less room

00:03:19.832 --> 00:03:23.102
between the outside of you
and your kidney.

00:03:23.102 --> 00:03:25.571
-There's less room between the
outside of me and my kidneys

00:03:25.571 --> 00:03:27.840
than probably anybody else
on planet Earth.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:30.309
[ Laughter ]
I've made peace with that.

00:03:30.309 --> 00:03:32.578
-A cool story has been
Brock Purdy,

00:03:32.578 --> 00:03:34.780
last player drafted
in the NFL draft last year.

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:36.716
Mr. Irrelevant is the name
they give to that

00:03:36.716 --> 00:03:39.318
and now, he's got --
the San Francisco 49ers

00:03:39.318 --> 00:03:41.587
in the championship game.
I feel like you keep waiting

00:03:41.587 --> 00:03:44.757
for it to strike midnight
and him to turn back

00:03:44.757 --> 00:03:46.659
into, you know,
regular old Cinderella,

00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:48.460
but what's going on here?
-I do hope that he has a measure

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:49.762
of perspective about this.

00:03:49.762 --> 00:03:52.364
Like you mentioned me being
an Emmy Award winner.

00:03:52.364 --> 00:03:54.100
I didn't win an Emmy.

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:55.301
I got an Emmy.

00:03:55.301 --> 00:03:56.869
-[ Laughs ]
-I was an executive producer

00:03:56.869 --> 00:04:00.072
on a "30 for 30" documentary
and so, when they do that

00:04:00.072 --> 00:04:02.141
and it wins
Best Documentary Series,

00:04:02.141 --> 00:04:03.609
everybody gets an Emmy.

00:04:03.609 --> 00:04:05.811
They would've won an Emmy,
no matter what I had done,

00:04:05.811 --> 00:04:07.347
and that makes it better.
-Right.

00:04:07.347 --> 00:04:08.914
[ Laughter ]
-Because I just got it, right?

00:04:08.914 --> 00:04:10.883
Like I didn't have to really --
I did something,

00:04:10.883 --> 00:04:12.251
but I just got it.

00:04:12.251 --> 00:04:13.886
And I hope that Brock Purdy
has the proper perspective

00:04:13.886 --> 00:04:16.522
to understand that,
while he is doing his job,

00:04:16.522 --> 00:04:18.624
if they win a Super Bowl,
he gonna get one.

00:04:18.624 --> 00:04:20.760
Like we don't have to make it
more than it actually is.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.294
We can appreciate that.
"Wow, you showed up

00:04:22.294 --> 00:04:24.130
for work late
and, the next thing you know,

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:26.064
everything went well
and you got a win?

00:04:26.064 --> 00:04:27.666
Great for you!" right?

00:04:27.666 --> 00:04:31.003
[ Laughter ]
But you gonna get that Emmy.

00:04:31.003 --> 00:04:35.374
-You had a great piece
about how crypto became

00:04:35.374 --> 00:04:37.543
very tied in
with professional sports,

00:04:37.543 --> 00:04:39.845
up to and including
the Miami Heat had

00:04:39.845 --> 00:04:41.981
an FTX Arena that they were
playing in.

00:04:41.981 --> 00:04:44.684
Obviously, they're no longer
playing in an FTX Arena,

00:04:44.684 --> 00:04:47.052
due to everything
that happened with FTX.

00:04:47.052 --> 00:04:48.421
[ Laughter ]
What was your original --

00:04:48.421 --> 00:04:50.790
Obviously, I think you were
very early here in saying,

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:52.525
"Hey, this doesn't quite
smell right.

00:04:52.525 --> 00:04:54.861
The whole world of crypto
and how quickly sports

00:04:54.861 --> 00:04:56.261
is wanting to have
a partnership with it."

00:04:56.261 --> 00:04:58.664
-Well, I had started looking
into it about ten years ago.

00:04:58.664 --> 00:05:00.800
There was this website
called Silk Road

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:04.837
that was basically an open air
drug market on the dark web.

00:05:04.837 --> 00:05:07.439
But real drug dealers
don't sell drugs on the Web,

00:05:07.439 --> 00:05:09.141
so you knew it wasn't
real drug dealers,

00:05:09.141 --> 00:05:10.909
but they were making
all their trades with Bitcoin

00:05:10.909 --> 00:05:12.311
and then the FBI showed up

00:05:12.311 --> 00:05:14.212
and they had all these not
actual drug dealers panicked.

00:05:14.212 --> 00:05:16.315
And I was like, "Well, tell me
more about this Bitcoin."

00:05:16.315 --> 00:05:19.718
And then I raised the question,
"Why would I trade my money

00:05:19.718 --> 00:05:21.487
for money that's not money,"
right?

00:05:21.487 --> 00:05:23.022
[ Laughter ]
Like when I go
out of the country,

00:05:23.022 --> 00:05:26.392
I take my money
and I trade it for other money.

00:05:26.392 --> 00:05:28.126
You're telling me
that I got this money

00:05:28.126 --> 00:05:29.762
that works perfectly well

00:05:29.762 --> 00:05:31.897
and I'm going to trade this
for some other money

00:05:31.897 --> 00:05:33.766
because this other money
might be worth more.

00:05:33.766 --> 00:05:36.235
Well, what's going to make the
value of that other money go up?

00:05:36.235 --> 00:05:37.937
"I'm going to tweet about it,"
right?

00:05:37.937 --> 00:05:40.139
[ Laughter ]
And we're just going
to make the value

00:05:40.139 --> 00:05:41.406
of this other money come up.

00:05:41.406 --> 00:05:42.741
And that was just like --

00:05:42.741 --> 00:05:44.644
I hear about like the digital
dollar and everything

00:05:44.644 --> 00:05:46.546
and I've studied economics
pretty decently and I was like,

00:05:46.546 --> 00:05:48.648
"My dollar look pretty damn
digital right now."

00:05:48.648 --> 00:05:50.716
[ Laughter ]
Like I got --

00:05:50.716 --> 00:05:52.852
the money in my pocket
is not all the money I have.

00:05:52.852 --> 00:05:54.620
The rest of it is ones
and zeros.

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:55.954
And I just couldn't see
how other people

00:05:55.954 --> 00:05:57.590
didn't understand the idea

00:05:57.590 --> 00:06:00.526
of turning your real money
into funny money

00:06:00.526 --> 00:06:01.994
[ Laughter ]
doesn't add up.

00:06:01.994 --> 00:06:04.497
-And you sort of saw this
very clearly

00:06:04.497 --> 00:06:05.698
and I should give you credit.

00:06:05.698 --> 00:06:06.999
A lot of people didn't.

00:06:06.999 --> 00:06:09.134
In the Super Bowl ads
because there were all these

00:06:09.134 --> 00:06:11.404
crypto ads last year
in the Super Bowl

00:06:11.404 --> 00:06:12.872
and they had
a very different message

00:06:12.872 --> 00:06:15.908
than sort of the more classic
financial services companies

00:06:15.908 --> 00:06:17.844
that have Super Bowl ads.
-Yeah, no, you see a commercial

00:06:17.844 --> 00:06:20.512
for Merrill Lynch
or for Charles Schwab

00:06:20.512 --> 00:06:21.981
or any of them other dorks that

00:06:21.981 --> 00:06:24.016
[ Laughter ]
get up there and do the trading

00:06:24.016 --> 00:06:25.684
and they're always telling you,

00:06:25.684 --> 00:06:26.986
[ Baritone ]
"Take care of your future."

00:06:26.986 --> 00:06:29.788
It's always very serene
and peaceful.

00:06:29.788 --> 00:06:33.259
These crypto ads were like,
"Fortune favors the brave!"

00:06:33.259 --> 00:06:35.761
Why do I need to be brave
about my money?

00:06:35.761 --> 00:06:39.632
[ Laughter ]
My entire life is about not
being brave about my money.

00:06:39.632 --> 00:06:41.867
The whole reason
that you take your money

00:06:41.867 --> 00:06:44.703
to somebody to invest it is
because you're not brave!

00:06:44.703 --> 00:06:47.639
[ Laughter ]
It is because you are terrified
of a zillion different factors

00:06:47.639 --> 00:06:49.542
and the only people
who are trying to be brave

00:06:49.542 --> 00:06:50.943
with their money are broke!

00:06:50.943 --> 00:06:53.445
[ Laughter ]
So, I understood why,
if you were broke

00:06:53.445 --> 00:06:55.714
and somebody tells you
you can turn five into ten,

00:06:55.714 --> 00:06:57.015
you'll give it a try.

00:06:57.015 --> 00:07:00.219
But, as our good friend
Mr. Madoff showed us,

00:07:00.219 --> 00:07:02.755
[ Laughter ]
once you hear about a certain
level of return,

00:07:02.755 --> 00:07:05.558
if it were real,
everybody would do it.

00:07:05.558 --> 00:07:07.660
Like you think about this --
when has there ever been a time

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:10.029
that somebody has been making
a lot of money off an investment

00:07:10.029 --> 00:07:11.364
and they made commercials
about it?

00:07:11.364 --> 00:07:13.698
[ Laughter ]

00:07:13.698 --> 00:07:14.967
It doesn't work that way!

00:07:14.967 --> 00:07:16.802
Nobody cares
that much about you!

00:07:16.802 --> 00:07:19.905
[ Laughter ]
-You mean, once they're telling
me, that proves it's not real?

00:07:19.905 --> 00:07:21.406
-That's all I'm saying.
When they are coming

00:07:21.406 --> 00:07:23.575
to offer this to you like,
"Hey, guys, here's a way

00:07:23.575 --> 00:07:26.312
that everybody can get rich,"
no one has ever

00:07:26.312 --> 00:07:30.449
[ Laughter ]
positioned anything to tell us
how we all can get rich.

00:07:30.449 --> 00:07:32.451
It doesn't go that way.
-Well, I still think

00:07:32.451 --> 00:07:34.120
it's pretty good and I'm putting
all my money into it.

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:35.387
[ Laughter ]

00:07:35.387 --> 00:07:36.656
Hey, thank you so much
for being here.

00:07:36.656 --> 00:07:38.057
-Hey, man, I appreciate it.
-I really appreciate it.

00:07:38.057 --> 00:07:39.391
[ Cheering and applause ]
Bomani Jones, everybody.

00:07:39.391 --> 00:07:40.860
New episodes of "Game Theory"

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:43.128
premiere Friday nights
on HBO and HBO Max.

00:07:43.128 --> 00:07:45.064
We'll be right back
with Sam Morrison.

